###  https://www.cobrain-project.eu/ontology/cobrain#CGS
:CGS rdf:type owl:Class ;
     rdfs:subClassOf :ThermalSpraying ;
     owl:hasKey ( :processID
                ) ;
     rdfs:comment """There are three variants of cold spraying:
- Low-pressure cold spray utilizes compressed air at pressures up to 10 bar and temperatures up to 600 °C. The powder is injected radially in the divergent part of the nozzle, using a pressureless powder feeder.
- High-pressure cold-spray utilizes nitrogen or helium at pressures up to 6 MPa and temperatures up to 1100 °C. The powder is injected axially in the cold spray torch, upstream of the nozzle, using a pressurized powder feeder.
- Medium-pressure cold spray operates with compressed air at intermediate pressure levels, usually in the range of 7 - 35 bar, with temperatures up to 550 °C - 600 °C. The powder is injected radially in the convergent part of the nozzle using a presureless powder feeder."""@en ;
     rdfs:isDefinedBy "ISO 14917:2017: Thermal Spraying - Terminology, classification." ;
     rdfs:seeAlso "Gartner F, Stoltenhoff T, Schmidt T, Kreye H (2006) The cold spray process and its potential  for industrial applications. J Therm Spray Technol 15(2):223–232" ,
                  "J. Karthikeyan (2013), Cold Spray Process, in: R.C. Tucker jr. (Ed.), ASM Handbook Vol. 5A: Thermal Spray Technology, ASM International, Materials Park, OH, USA." ;
     <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "Cold Gas Dynamic Spraying"@en ,
                                                    "Cold Gas Spraying"@en ,
                                                    "Cold Spraying"@en ,
                                                    "Kinetic Spraying"@en ;
     <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "CGS"@en ;
     :elucidation "A thermal spray process utilizing a supersonic jet of produced by the expansion of a compressed through a converging/diverging nozzle gas to accelerate, at or near room temperature, powder particles to high velocities. The solid particles traveling at speeds often above 500 m/s plastically deform and consolidate on impact with their substrate to create a coating."@en .


